.TH SORT 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sort \- sort lines of text files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sort
.RB [ \-bCcdfghiMmnRrsuVz ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR keydef ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR char ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sort
reads all lines of the named files (standard input if none is given or
a
.I file
is
.BR \- ),
sorts them together and writes the result to standard output.
A last line without a terminating newline is treated as a complete line
and gets a newline on output.
.PP
All input is read into memory before any output is produced, so
.B \-o
may name one of the input files.
.PP
Without a key option, lines are compared as byte strings (as with
.BR memcmp (3));
no locale collation is performed.
Lines that compare equal appear in an unspecified order unless
.B \-s
is given; there is no final whole-line comparison.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be combined in one argument
.RB ( \-nr ).
Option parsing stops at the first argument that does not begin with
.BR \- ,
at a lone
.BR \- ,
or after
.BR \-\- .
.SS Ordering
Only one of
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-M ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-R ,
.B \-V
is in effect; the last one given wins.
.TP
.B \-n
Numeric: compare the leading decimal integer (optional sign) of each
key.
Leading white space is skipped.
Fractions, thousands separators and exponents are not recognised; a
key with no digits sorts as 0.
Values that overflow saturate at the largest or smallest
.BR "long long" .
.TP
.B \-g
General numeric: compare values parsed with
.BR strtod (3),
so decimals and exponents are handled.
.TP
.B \-h
Human numeric: like
.B \-g
but a suffix
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.B T
or
.B P
(either case) immediately after the number multiplies it by the
corresponding power of 1024.
.TP
.B \-M
Month: compare the first three characters of the key, case-insensitively,
as
.B JAN
<
.B FEB
< ... <
.BR DEC .
Anything else sorts before
.BR JAN .
.TP
.B \-V
Version: runs of digits are compared by numeric value and other
characters byte by byte, so
.B foo\-1.9
sorts before
.BR foo\-1.10 .
.TP
.B \-R
Random: order lines by a hash of the first 32 bytes of their key,
seeded from the current time.
Identical keys end up next to each other.
.SS Modifiers
.TP
.B \-b
Ignore leading blanks (spaces and tabs) of the key.
.TP
.B \-d
Dictionary order: only alphanumeric and white-space characters take
part in the comparison.
.TP
.B \-f
Fold lower case to upper case for comparison.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore non-printable characters.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the result of each comparison.
.PP
.BR \-d ,
.B \-f
and
.B \-i
apply to the byte-string comparison;
.B \-b
also affects the numeric and month orderings.
.SS Keys and fields
.TP
.BI \-t " char"
Fields are separated by the single character
.IR char .
Only its first character is used; an empty argument is an error.
Without
.BR \-t ,
fields are separated by runs of spaces and tabs, and leading blanks
are not part of the first field.
.TP
.BI \-k " keydef"
Compare only the key described by
.IR keydef ,
which has the form
.IR field1 [ .char1 ][ ,field2 [ .char2 ]],
all numbers counting from 1.
The key starts at character
.I char1
of field
.I field1
(the start of the field if omitted) and ends at character
.I char2
of field
.I field2
(the end of that field if
.I char2
is omitted or 0), or at the end of the line if
.I field2
is omitted.
Only one key is supported; if
.B \-k
is repeated, the last one is used.
Ordering letters written after a field position (such as
.BR 2n )
are accepted but ignored; the global options apply to the key.
.SS Other options
.TP
.B \-c
Check whether the input is already sorted instead of sorting it.
On the first line out of order, print a
.B disorder
message naming that line and exit with status 1.
.TP
.B \-C
Like
.BR \-c ,
but print nothing.
.TP
.B \-m
Accepted for compatibility.
The input is sorted in full anyway, which gives the same result for
sorted input.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write the result to
.I output
instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-s
Stable sort: lines with equal keys keep their input order.
.TP
.B \-u
Unique: of each group of lines that compare equal (on the key, if one
is given), write only the first.
.TP
.B \-z
Lines are terminated by NUL bytes instead of newlines, on both input
and output.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message to standard error and exit with status 2.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C ,
the input was sorted.
.TP
.B 1
With
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C ,
the input was not sorted.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, a file could not be opened, the output file
could not be created, or memory ran out.
Unreadable input files are reported and skipped; the others are still
sorted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Sort
.I /etc/passwd
by numeric user ID:
.EX
$ sort \-t : \-k 3,3 \-n /etc/passwd
.EE
.PP
Sort sizes such as 10K and 2M, largest first:
.EX
$ sort \-hr sizes
.EE
.PP
Remove duplicate lines in place:
.EX
$ sort \-u \-o list list
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR join (1),
.BR tr (1),
.BR wc (1),
.BR qsort (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The ordering and key options follow POSIX.1-2024
.BR sort ;
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-M ,
.BR \-R ,
.B \-V
and
.B \-z
are BSD and GNU extensions.
.SH BUGS
Only a single
.B \-k
key is supported, and per-key ordering letters are ignored.
.PP
.B \-m
does not merge; it sorts.
.PP
With
.B \-c
the disorder message always names the input
.B \-
and a line number counted across all inputs, and a file that could not
be opened does not change the exit status.
.PP
Errors while reading input or writing the output are not detected.
